WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Leak prevention starts with ownership.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:10.840
Ownership answers a practical question: who is responsible for keeping this resource alive and releasing it when the work is done?

3
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:21.100
A design with one clear owner is usually easier to understand, test, and maintain than a design where responsibility moves by convention.

4
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.620
Borrowing is different from ownership.

5
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:32.520
A borrowed pointer, reference, span, iterator, or view lets code use a resource temporarily without taking cleanup responsibility.

6
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.860
Borrowed access should remain within the lifetime contract of the owner.

7
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.720
It should not silently become a long-lived dependency.

8
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.560
Ownership transfer should be visible in code.

9
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:49.660
If a function creates a resource and gives responsibility to the caller, the return type should express that.

10
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.760
If a function accepts responsibility from the caller, the parameter type and call site should make the handoff obvious.

11
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.300
Hidden transfer rules create fragile cleanup behavior.

12
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.300
Shared ownership requires deliberate design.

13
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.080
When multiple owners can keep a resource alive, cleanup happens only after the final owner releases it.

14
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:16.100
That can be correct, but reviewers need to understand who may extend lifetime, how cycles are prevented, and what resources may remain retained.

15
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.380
Cleanup rules should be easy to review.

16
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:26.560
Constructors, destructors, member types, function signatures, and wrapper types should communicate lifecycle responsibilities.

17
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:32.260
Comments can help, but the design should not depend on comments to decide who releases a resource.

18
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:38.140
Ambiguous lifetime rules create leak risk because each component may assume another component will clean up.

19
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:46.160
During review, ask who creates, observes, stores, transfers, shares, and destroys each resource.

20
00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.700
If the answer is unclear, the design needs more lifecycle information in the code itself.

21
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:56.940
One practical technique is to document ownership at module boundaries.

22
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:06.080
Public APIs should make it clear whether callers keep responsibility, give responsibility away, receive a borrowed view, or participate in shared lifetime management.
